# Laravel Production-Ready Docker Images

A reference/copy-paste repository providing stateless, performant Docker images for Laravel, designed for Kubernetes and deployed via Convox. Compatible with Laravel Sail for local development.

## What This Is

This is **not** a runnable application. It is a collection of Dockerfiles, configs, and scripts that users copy into their own Laravel projects. There is no Laravel app in this repo.

## Supported Engines

| Engine     | Dockerfile            | Proxy  | Notes                                    |
| ---------- | --------------------- | ------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| fpm        | `Dockerfile`          | nginx  | Traditional PHP-FPM behind nginx         |
| roadrunner | `Dockerfile`          | nginx  | Octane + RoadRunner behind nginx          |
| swoole     | `Dockerfile`          | nginx  | Octane + Swoole behind nginx              |
| frankenphp | `Dockerfile.frankenphp` | Caddy  | Octane + FrankenPHP (official image, no nginx) |

Each engine supports two build targets: `production` and `development`.

## Key Decisions

- **Alpine Linux 3.24** for nginx-based images (fpm, roadrunner, swoole). FrankenPHP uses Debian because musl libc is slower with PHP ZTS mode.
- **supervisord** manages processes in nginx-based images. In production: nginx + php-fpm/octane. In development: nginx only, plus a single `dev` program running `php artisan dev` — the app server (php-fpm/octane) and the sidecars (queue listener, scheduler, Vite, Pail) are all dev processes, consistent across engines. The engine supervisor confs inherited from the engine stage are removed in the development stage (`RUN rm`).
- **`php artisan dev` for dev sidecars** — requires Laravel 13.16+ (13.25+ recommended: auto-selects inline output without a TTY) and Node >= 22.13 (`@laravel/multiplex` floor). Which processes run is defined in the app (a `DevServiceProvider` — snippet in the README's "Development" section), not in the image.
- **FrankenPHP has no supervisord** — in development `php artisan dev` is the container CMD (same as every engine, just without nginx). Production CMD is still `octane:start` directly.
- **php-fpm under multiplex needs `2>&1 | cat`** — php-fpm opens its `error_log` (`/dev/stderr`) as a path, which fails with ENXIO when stderr is a unix socket (Node `spawn` uses socketpairs for stdio). The pipe makes stderr a pipe, which `/dev/stderr` can open. Safe because multiplex spawns with `detached: true` and signals the whole process group. `-O`/`--force-stderr` does NOT help (the error_log open happens at config time regardless).
- **Node.js in the FrankenPHP image** — copied from `node:24-trixie-slim` (`COPY --from`, replaces the apt `npm` package) because Debian 13 apt pins Node at 20.x, below the multiplex floor. Alpine images need nothing: Alpine 3.24 ships Node 24.
- **No CI pipeline** — verification is done via a Cursor SKILL (`.cursor/skills/verify-docker-images/`) that builds all 8 image variants, starts containers, and runs a comprehensive checklist.
- **Development images** don't bake in source code; they expect a bind-mount. Production images copy everything in.
- **FrankenPHP** is exposed directly (no nginx proxy), unlike the other engines. Port remapped from 8000 to 8080 for consistency.
- **`OCTANE_PORT` env** — declared per image (8000 in the nginx-based octane stage, since nginx owns 8080; 8080 in the FrankenPHP image). Consumed by the production `octane.conf` supervisor program and by the app's DevServiceProvider snippet. The FrankenPHP production CMD keeps `--port=8080` hardcoded (exec form can't expand env vars).
- **All processes run as non-root UID 1000** — nginx, php-fpm, octane, and supervisor all share the same UID. FPM socket ownership is left to the creating process (no `listen.owner`/`listen.group`) since everything runs as the same user.
- **FrankenPHP ownership** — `/config` and `/data` (Caddy + XDG directories) are chowned to UID 1000. The FrankenPHP image sets `XDG_CONFIG_HOME=/config`, so PsySH and other XDG-aware tools write there.
- **RoadRunner binary** — downloaded at build time for production, at startup for development (inside the container to match the Linux architecture).
- **php-fpm `catch_workers_output = no`** — intentional. PHP errors reach container logs via the FastCGI STDERR stream (`fastcgi.logging = On` by default). With `catch_workers_output = yes`, FPM additionally pipes worker output to its own error log, causing duplicate entries. The `no` setting avoids this duplication.
- **npm and git in production images** — kept intentionally. They add ~30-40MB on Alpine but are useful for operational debugging when SSH'ing into running containers (e.g. `composer require`, `npm rebuild`).
- **`--mount=type=cache`** — used for Composer and npm caches during build. Caches are mounted at build time but never written into image layers, keeping images smaller and speeding up repeat builds. Requires Docker BuildKit (already required by `COPY --link`).
- **`migrate --force --isolated=1`** — `--isolated` makes the container that loses the lock SKIP the migration, not wait (verified in `Illuminate\Console\Command::execute()`: it prints "The [migrate] command is already running." and returns the option value, or `$isolatedExitCode = self::SUCCESS` when no value is given). A bare `--isolated` therefore boots the loser against a possibly mid-migration schema. `=1` exits non-zero so `set -e` stops the entrypoint and the orchestrator restarts the container — the retry IS the wait. `1` collides with a genuinely failed migration's exit code; that is fine, because both must stop the boot. No blanket fallback to an unlocked `migrate` on that exit code: Laravel gives no distinct code for "lock busy" vs. "lock store missing" vs. "migration failed" — they're all the generic failure exit — so a catch-all `||` would just as happily fire on ordinary lock contention (defeating `--isolated` on every normal multi-replica boot) as on a genuinely broken migration (silently re-running it unlocked). `75` (`EX_TEMPFAIL`) was considered and dropped — it only existed to feed that same catch-all. The one legitimate bootstrap case (brand-new database, no `migrations` table, so there is nothing to lock against — `--isolated` fails regardless of cache store) is instead detected narrowly and upfront with `php artisan migrate:status`, which exits non-zero exactly when that table doesn't exist; only then does the entrypoint fall back to a plain `migrate --force`, which also creates the `cache`/`cache_locks` tables if `CACHE_STORE=database`. Every later boot takes the isolated path. Residual caveat, inherent to bootstrapping any lock from nothing: if the very first deploy itself starts with multiple replicas, more than one can see the same missing table and race on the same unlocked bootstrap migration — deploy the first release with a single replica. Stricter alternative in the README: migrate once per release from CI and delete the block.
- **Migration policy is an explicit environment variable** — `start-container.sh` (`ENTRYPOINT`) does per-container prep for every process (local deps, migrations when `RUN_MIGRATIONS=true`, production `optimize`, which must run everywhere because `bootstrap/cache` is container-local). Dockerfiles default `RUN_MIGRATIONS=true` so ordinary Docker/Sail starts behave as before. Convox services state their policy visibly: `app=true`, `worker=false`, `scheduler=false`. A purpose-specific switch is simpler and less error-prone than deriving migration behavior from a generic role or Convox's injected `SERVICE`, and works identically outside Convox. `convox run app` inherits the app service setting, so it migrates; supply `RUN_MIGRATIONS=false` for harmless one-off commands.
- **Release identity is the git tag, else the commit SHA** — `stubs/config/deployment.php`, copied to the app's `config/deployment.php`. `GIT_TAG` is a build arg next to `COMMIT_SHA` (GitLab: `CI_COMMIT_TAG`, empty on branch pipelines). A tag becomes Sentry `package@version` (`Str::slug(APP_NAME).'@'.ltrim(tag, 'v')`); no tag means the bare SHA. Do not use `git describe` (an ancestor tag plus a distance lies about what was built) and do not use Convox `RELEASE` (a new release id for an unchanged commit whenever env changes). `env()` inside config files is baked by `config:cache` and works; `env()` in app code returns null afterwards, so Sentry reads `config('deployment.release')`. `deployment.php` loads before `sentry.php` alphabetically, so that `config()` call resolves during caching.
- **One cache store for every shared flag** — `convox.yml` sets `APP_MAINTENANCE_STORE=` (empty, not omitted). Stock `config/app.php` defaults it to `'database'`, but `MaintenanceModeManager::createCacheDriver()` uses `config('app.maintenance.store') ?: config('cache.default')`, and `Env::get` returns `''` for a set-but-empty var, so it falls back to `CACHE_STORE` — the same store as queue pauses, schedule pauses, scheduler locks and `--isolated`. The `KEY=` form is a manifest default, so Convox never requires `convox env set` for it. `compose.yaml` doesn't set it; locally Laravel's own default applies.
- **No `optimize:clear` at startup** — `getOptimizeClearTasks()` includes `'cache' => 'cache:clear'`, and `RedisStore::flush()` is `$this->connection()->flushdb()` — a whole-database wipe, NOT a prefix-aware delete. Every booting pod would flush the shared Redis cache mid-rollout. Stock Laravel limits the blast radius by putting cache on the `cache` connection (`REDIS_CACHE_DB=1`) and locks on `default` via `'lock_connection' => env('REDIS_CACHE_LOCK_CONNECTION', 'default')`, but managed Redis exposing only database `0` collapses cache, sessions, queues and locks onto one database. Clearing is pointless anyway: a fresh container starts with an empty `bootstrap/cache`. `optimize` itself stays — it writes `bootstrap/cache/*.php` on the container's own filesystem, unrelated to `CACHE_STORE`, so every container must run it and there is no shared state to inherit.
- **`filament:optimize`** — commented out in `start-container.sh`. Users of Filament should uncomment it. Without the guard, `set -eu` would crash container startup for any project not using Filament.
- **Vite dev server** — always runs in development via `artisan dev`. To disable it in a project, use `DevCommands::except('vite')` in the app's DevServiceProvider — no Dockerfile change needed.
- **No `npm run build` at container start** — `start-container.sh` only runs `npm install` in local; assets are served by the Vite dev server. Production builds happen at image build time.
- **Fresh-clone/worktree boot gotchas** (verified live on a real project): because `start-container.sh` runs `composer install` at container start, anything the stack needs BEFORE that must not live in `vendor/`. Sail's pgsql stub mounts `./vendor/laravel/sail/database/pgsql/create-testing-database.sql` into initdb — on a fresh clone Docker creates the missing host path as a directory, initdb fails, pgsql exits, and the app's migrate then dies on unresolvable `pgsql` host. Fix: vendor the 2-line SQL into the repo (e.g. `docker/pgsql/`) and mount that. Also: gitignored secrets (`.env`, `auth.json`) don't travel to worktrees — `COMPOSER_AUTH` is injected as a runtime env var in compose.yaml (not just a build arg) precisely so composer works without `auth.json`; projects with separate worker containers should have the worker wait for a boot marker (e.g. `storage/framework/.entrypoint-ready` touched by the web entrypoint after migrate), not just for `vendor/autoload.php` — the autoloader appears before migrate finishes and the worker crash-loops into supervisord FATAL otherwise.
- **No `tty: true` in compose.yaml** — container processes run under supervisord (or `artisan dev` for FrankenPHP) and never have a TTY; `docker compose exec` allocates its own.
- **Local dev domains via caddy-docker-proxy** — one shared [caddy-docker-proxy](https://github.com/lucaslorentz/caddy-docker-proxy) per machine (ports 80/443, external `caddy` network), started with the `docker run` one-liner in the README (no proxy files in this repo). App containers declare domains via labels (`caddy`, `caddy.reverse_proxy: {{upstreams 8080}}`, plus a `caddy_1` group for Vite on 5173). No Caddyfile, no network aliases, no cert management: the plugin builds routes from the Docker socket and Caddy's internal CA mints one cert per hostname. No `tls internal` label — the internal CA is the automatic issuer for non-public names like `.localhost`. The CA root persists in the proxy's data volume; users trust it once. Note: the root cert path inside the container is `/data/caddy/pki/authorities/local/root.crt` (XDG_DATA_HOME=/data + `caddy/` app dir). The proxy self-labels (`proxy.localhost` + `caddy.respond: OK`) to force CA creation at startup (it's otherwise lazy) and to provide a health check for the DNS+TLS+trust chain.
- **Single domain source: the project folder name** — compose injects `APP_URL`, `SESSION_DOMAIN` and `VITE_DEV_SERVER_URL` derived from `${COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME}` (verified with `docker compose config`); container env wins over `.env` in Laravel (phpdotenv is immutable), so `.env` stays 100% stock — zero per-project values, zero worktree edits, and a copied `.env` can never collide two stacks. An intermediate design injected a host-only `APP_DOMAIN` and had `.env` derive `APP_URL=https://${APP_DOMAIN}` — reverted: Compose eagerly expands nested `${VAR}` refs in `.env` values at load time and can't see container-injected vars, so every compose/sail command printed `The "APP_DOMAIN" variable is not set` (verified live). Also verified: the warning fires even for `.env` values that compose.yaml never references (expansion is unconditional), and `.env` cannot reference `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` at all (parsed before the project name is resolved — expands to blank). Nested defaults like `${APP_DOMAIN:-${COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME}.localhost}` DO work (verified) but make lines longer, not DRYer. Conclusion: repeating `${COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME}.localhost` in compose.yaml is the least-bad option; the DRY alternative (literal `APP_DOMAIN` in `.env`) breaks worktree zero-setup. Default is the apex domain (`myapp.localhost`, no `api.` prefix); multi-subdomain apps add hosts to the `caddy` label separated by `', '` (Caddy rejects comma-without-space — the whole site block is dropped, verified live) and point the compose `APP_URL` line at the canonical host. Projects whose domain ≠ folder name (e.g. naba: folder `naba-competition-be`, domain `naba.localhost`) should NOT use the injection — declare `APP_DOMAIN` in `.env` as the single source and interpolate labels from it. Caveat: host-side `php artisan` (outside the container) sees only stock `.env` values — the workflow is sail-only. Auxiliary HTTP services (Mailpit, MinIO/RustFS console, …) get plain `caddy` labels on their own service, hostname prefixed by the service name (`mailpit.${COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME}.localhost`) — the `caddy_N` index is per-container, only needed when one service serves multiple domains (app + Vite). Object storage: also publish the S3 API port (typically 9000), not just the console (9001) — browser-to-bucket uploads (presigned PUT/POST) hit the API; same for MinIO and RustFS.
- **Service-name DNS leaks onto the shared `caddy` network** — Compose auto-registers the bare service name as a DNS alias on EVERY network a service joins (no opt-in), so two projects with a `mailpit` service both attached to `caddy` make `mailpit` resolve to two IPs round-robin for any container on both networks (the app) → silent cross-project delivery (hit live on a Strapi project: half the mail landed in another project's Mailpit, SMTP 250 either way, zero logs). Convention: auxiliary services declare a manual alias (`mailpit.internal`) ONLY on the project network (`sail`) and clients point at it (`MAIL_HOST: 'mailpit.internal'` injected via the app's compose `environment` block — container env wins, `.env` stays stock); manual aliases exist only where declared and project networks are per-project/worktree, so resolution is always unambiguous. The alias MUST be a different name than the service: re-declaring `mailpit` as a manual alias is a no-op because the auto-registered service-name alias on `caddy` can't be removed — there is NO opt-out in Compose (upstream feature request [docker/compose#8223](https://github.com/docker/compose/issues/8223) open since 2021; fix PR [#13784](https://github.com/docker/compose/pull/13784), which would have skipped service-name aliases on external networks like ours, was closed unmerged; behavior verified unchanged on Compose v5.5.0). Never declare the alias on `caddy` — that attachment exists only so the proxy can reach the container for its UI labels. `.internal` is just a convention meaning "container-to-container only", not a Docker feature. Also delete mailpit's `ports` (`1025`/`8025`): SMTP is container-to-container and the UI goes through the proxy — this also unblocks parallel worktree stacks (hardcoded host ports otherwise collide).
- **Trusted proxies** — both Caddy locally and Convox's LB/ingress in production terminate TLS and forward `X-Forwarded-Proto/For/Host`; Laravel's `TrustProxies` ignores them until proxies are trusted. Guide recommends `trustProxies(at: '*')` (safe because containers are only reachable via the proxy once ports are unpublished), with private CIDRs as the stricter alternative. Convox caveat: real client IP requires the rack param `proxy_protocol=true` (default false, 5–10 min downtime, incompatible with `internal_router`; pair with `disable_convox_resolver=true` when using internal service routing). Proto/Host headers work without it.
- **Worktree domains: `myapp-<worktree>.localhost`** (dash inside the parent). Per-host certs mean any depth works TLS-wise — the dash-parent shape is chosen for cookie isolation: `myapp-<worktree>.localhost` is its own registrable domain (`localhost` is not on the PSL), while the dotted `myapp.<worktree>.localhost` has registrable domain `<worktree>.localhost`, shareable across projects. Verified with a live smoke test (label routing, per-host certs, 4th-level domains all pass). Worktrees rebuild the image on first `sail up` because Compose tags build images `<project>-<service>` (project = dir name) — expected and cheap (BuildKit cache is content-addressed, machine-wide); do NOT pin a shared `image:` tag (stale-image + tag-overwrite hazards) or `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` (collides everything).
- **Published ports become optional with the proxy** — the compose example keeps them for backwards compatibility but tells users to delete them; unpublished ports are what make parallel worktree stacks collision-free. Other Sail services (MySQL, Redis) still publish `FORWARD_*` ports and must be unpublished or remapped per worktree.
- **Vite through the proxy** — the only required `vite.config.js` line is `server.origin: process.env.VITE_DEV_SERVER_URL`: without it, laravel-vite-plugin writes `http://localhost:5173` into the `hot` file (its Sail-aware default), unreachable once ports are unpublished. `host: 0.0.0.0` is already the plugin default under `LARAVEL_SAIL=1` (set by Sail's compose stub), and Vite core allows `*.localhost` hosts and CORS by default — `allowedHosts`/`cors` config is redundant. HMR rides through Caddy over wss on the `vite.${APP_DOMAIN}` host.
- **Safari caveat** — `*.localhost` resolves natively in Chrome/Firefox/Edge and system-wide on macOS 26+; older macOS needs dnsmasq + `/etc/resolver/localhost` for Safari.
- **xdebug.ini** — contains `zend_extension=xdebug.so` and config directives (`xdebug.mode=debug`). Alpine's `php-xdebug` package does NOT auto-create a conf.d loader, so the `zend_extension` line is required. For FrankenPHP, `install-php-extensions` also loads xdebug — the duplicate `zend_extension` is harmless (PHP skips already-loaded extensions). Both Dockerfiles COPY the same config file.
- **supervisord restart policy: `autorestart=true` + `startsecs=1` + `startretries=10` (all four programs) + `killasgroup=true` (all four) + `stopasgroup=true` (nginx, php-fpm, dev — NOT octane)**. The group flags make a stop or forced kill reach the workers, not only the master; otherwise the next start can fail to bind the socket or port and burn the fast-crash retries. Octane is the exception for `stopasgroup`: it drains its own server on TERM, and a group-wide TERM hits its PHP workers directly and drops in-flight requests (verified live: RoadRunner and Swoole both dropped a 10s request with it, both completed it without). Replaces the previous `autorestart=%(ENV_SUPERVISOR_AUTORESTART)s` + `startretries=0`, which was an outage trap: `startretries` ONLY counts exits that happen within `startsecs` (default 1s), so the first sub-second crash — exactly what a DB blip causes — sent the program to `FATAL` permanently while supervisord (and therefore the container) stayed up, invisible to the orchestrator. Verified live with a throwaway supervisord container: old settings = 1 start then `FATAL` forever; `startsecs=0`+`autorestart=true` = infinite restarts but a 28/sec hot loop; final settings = 8 backed-off attempts across a simulated 30s outage then automatic recovery to `RUNNING`, and exactly 11 attempts (1 + 10 retries, ~55s of linear 1s/2s/3s… backoff) before `FATAL` for a permanently broken program. `FATAL` is deliberate: in production the new `liveness` probe on `/up` (`failureThreshold: 6`, `interval: 10`) makes Kubernetes replace the pod with real exponential backoff; locally `restart: unless-stopped` in compose.yaml covers container exit (FrankenPHP's failure mode, no supervisord there). The `SUPERVISOR_AUTORESTART` env var was deleted — production and development now use identical restart semantics, so the knob encoded nothing.
- **Timeouts are nested, inner < outer** (README "Timeouts & Shutdown"). Web: PHP `max_execution_time` 60 (CPU time only on Linux) / php-fpm `request_terminate_timeout` 60s (the real wall-clock limit) / Octane `max_execution_time` 30 (app config; nginx `proxy_read_timeout` 3600 is for WebSockets) ≤ nginx `fastcgi_read_timeout` 60s default ≤ Convox `timeout` 60. App shutdown: request limit 60 < supervisord `stopwaitsecs=65`, and `preStop: sleep 5` + 65 < `termination.grace: 75`. Worker: `--timeout=60` < `termination.grace: 70`, both under Laravel's default `retry_after` 90 (equal timeout and retry_after lets a slow job run twice) — chosen as Laravel's own defaults so no `*_QUEUE_RETRY_AFTER` env vars are needed. Deploy time is a hard constraint: grace values are ceilings, not waits (idle pods/workers exit at once), and Convox's default `deployment.maximum: 200` stops old pods together, so a normal deploy pays the 5s `preStop` once. `preStop` was cut from 10 to 5 because it is the one fixed cost on every deploy; 5s covers ingress endpoint propagation. Reasoning lives in the README, not in config comments. Long jobs should implement `Interruptible` (Laravel 13.7+, daemon `queue:work` only; `queue:restart` never calls it) rather than inflate grace. The worker's `timeout:` was removed — Convox documents it as the request read/write timeout and the worker has no port.
- **Graceful drain uses `stopsignal=QUIT` for nginx and php-fpm** — both treat TERM (supervisord's default) as an immediate stop that drops in-flight requests, and supervisord's default `stopwaitsecs` is 10. Verified live with section P of the verify skill (10s request, `docker stop` 2s in): before, all three nginx-based engines dropped it in 1s; after, all three return 200 with an ~8–9s stop. php-fpm needed no `process_control_timeout` for this.
- **FrankenPHP `php` wrapper must `exec`** — `scripts/php.frankenphp.sh` is the container's PID 1 path (`start-container` execs `php artisan ...`). Without `exec`, bash is PID 1, ignores SIGTERM and never forwards it: every stop waited for the SIGKILL (90s in the test). Even with the signal delivered, FrankenPHP drops in-flight requests: Octane's signal handler calls Caddy's admin `/stop` and then `exit(0)` immediately, and PID 1 exiting kills Caddy ([laravel/octane#970](https://github.com/laravel/octane/issues/970)). No in-image fix without replacing Octane's start command; the `preStop` sleep is the mitigation. Octane on RoadRunner/Swoole survives the same pattern only because nginx (QUIT) keeps supervisord, and thus the container, alive until the proxied request completes.
- **Scheduler runs as a `scheduler` service (`php artisan schedule:work --whisper`), not a Convox Timer** — a Timer running `schedule:run` would map to a Kubernetes `CronJob`, paying a full container boot every minute (1,440/day) for a command that's usually a no-op; `schedule:work` is a long-lived Process that forks `schedule:run` in-process once a minute, which also enables sub-minute schedules (impossible with a Timer, which can't go below one minute). Deliberately NO `singleton: true`: Convox documents it as replace-before-start (old Process stopped before the new one starts), and `schedule:work` only fires on `second === 0` with no catch-up, so any minute boundary inside that gap is lost forever. The default "make one, break one" rolling update overlaps instead, and `Schedule::alwaysOnOneServer()` (README "Application Safeguards", requires a shared cache store — not `file`/`array`) is the PRIMARY duplicate guard: `CacheSchedulingMutex` locks per task per minute, so any number of concurrent `schedule:work` Processes run each due task once. Fixed `scale.count: 1` only avoids steady-state duplicate loops; it is not a correctness requirement. Ticks can still be lost if the scheduler is down (crash, node loss), so tasks should reconcile state rather than assume every tick ran. `--whisper` suppresses the "No scheduled commands are ready to run." noise logged every idle minute. Deliberately NOT using `--run-output-file`: it would redirect `schedule:run` output to a file instead of stdout, breaking the convention that container logs are the single source of truth. The README documents two further options (a `schedule:run` Timer with `concurrency: Forbid`; one generated Timer per task via `schedule:list --json`) as viable but more complex/expensive alternatives — not implemented here.

## Testing
Testing must be performed using the skills defined in the project and any change must be implemented with some sort of TDD through the usage of the skill
